The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". Its own table says two
causes. The reports say three problems. This page keeps what the data shows, and gives each
problem a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
Reading the 412 bodies, not the subjects, is what split the ticket. Decision 0001 merges on
subject alone, and twelve subject lines covered all three problems.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

How the rows are made, in words:

1. One id, one report. The export has 427 rows and 412 ids; the ticket system pages overlap
   (`uniqueById`, as `causes.ts` already does).
2. Every report is placed by its **tenant** (locale and booking form), never by what intake
   guessed from the browser (`causeOf` in `causes.ts`). Intake disagreed with the tenant on 38
   reports: 23 tagged `hint` are `parser`, 15 tagged `parser` are `hint`.
3. The five reports the desk itself read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) leave the `parser`
   row and get their own. `causes.ts` kept them under `parser` as a footnote; a report belongs to
   one problem, so here it is a row. That is why `parser` reads 374 here and 379 in the table below.

## The three problems

**`parser` · dates parse month first for day-first tenants.** The shared parser reads every date
as mm/dd. All eleven tenants on file write the day first (`dayFirst` asks the platform, per
locale), so 07/04 becomes 4 July. Every channel, every tenant, April to October. One code fix
in the parser.

**`hint` · booking-v3 prints the wrong hint under the date field.** Through `booking-v3` the form
tells people to type `MM/DD/YYYY`; they do, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first.
Only the three tenants on `booking-v3` (alder.physio, copper.studio, pale.harbour), only the
`form` channel. Fixing the parser does not fix this: the hint is wrong, not the parse.

**`one-day-move` · the day moves by one, the month does not.** Five reports describe a shift of
one day with the month intact, or a time-zone split: "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says
Monday 11 May", "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday", "The invite arrived
before I made the booking", "Every booking in June is one day off". Not a parse order problem. A
date is being treated as an instant and crossing midnight somewhere. Under-reported: the last one
says "I have stopped reporting these".

## What the desk's table shows today

For comparison, the table the current page builds from the same data:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What to change on the desk

- Mark #4471 as the `parser` problem only; link the other two tickets from it.
- Stop closing the ticket on subject matches alone for date problems: the body carries the
  problem, the subject does not. A new decision record should replace 0001 for this class.
- Intake's `intakeTag` is a guess from the browser locale. Place reports by tenant at intake,
  which the tenant directory already allows.

